sections.body_md:
The earliest factories in England appeared around the **1730s** and multiplied from the late eighteenth century. **Cotton** was the first symbol of the new era. A series of inventions speeded up each stage of production, and **Richard Arkwright** created the **cotton mill**, where costly machines and all processes were brought **under one roof and one management**, allowing supervision, quality control and regulation of labour.

**Change was slower than "revolution" suggests:**
- cotton led until the 1840s; then **iron and steel** grew with the **railways** (in England from the 1840s, in the colonies from the 1860s);
- most workers stayed in **traditional industries** (food processing, building, pottery, glass, tanning, furniture), which changed through small innovations;
- new machines were **expensive, unreliable and costly to repair**: **James Watt** improved Newcomen's steam engine and patented it in **1781**, and his partner **Matthew Boulton** manufactured it, but it spread slowly.
